  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 101 PM CST Wed Nov 11 2026\n\nWater vapor imagery shows northwest flow over the region following \nan initial trough axis that moved over last night. A secondary \ntrough axis is noted near Sioux City and is moving southward through \nthe mid-level flow. Northwest sfc winds are breezy this afternoon \nbehind an initial cold front. CAA strengthens tonight as secondary \ncold front moves through the area and temperatures fall into the \nupper 30s to near 40 degrees by sunrise Thursday. Low stratus may \nmove over the area for a brief period early tomorrow but sunshine \nreturns for the afternoon. A cool sfc ridge will continue to build \nsouthward into the region throughout the day and into early Friday. \nHighs tomorrow will range from the upper 40s in far northeast KS, to\nthe low 50s in central portions of the state. Temps fall into the \n20s area-wide Thursday night/early Friday and then struggle to reach \n50 degrees Friday afternoon.\n\nThe sfc ridge will slowly expand eastward on Saturday but the cool \nand dry air mass will remain in place over eastern KS. Attention then\nturns to the west where an elongated trough axis from the PNW toward\nthe Four Corners region splits, creating a closed low that moves \nover the OK/TX panhandles early Sunday. Trends during the last few \ndays have continued to take that system further south. The associated\ndeeper moisture now looks to stay well south of the area, with a low\nlevel ridge centered to our east keeping dry atmospheric conditions \nin place here. Thus, confidence has increased on maintaining dry \nweather through the weekend. NBM still has some elevated POPs, \nespecially in central KS but am thinking we will see that trend down\nwith subsequent runs. Temperatures stay cool into early next week, \nbut do warm slightly. Rain chances likely won't return until later \nnext week.",
